The six M=0 lists are 123,132,213,231,312,321. The seven M=1 lists are 112,113,122,131,212,221,311. The three M=2 lists are 111,121,211. Thus there are 16 successes and 11 failures, giving [6,7,3]. These cases were followed by hand from the vacancy rule, independently of the Lean cardinality computation and without using another task's results or a general enumeration formula.

Every declaration is in namespace Parking. Preferences is the labeled preference type; preferenceList converts it to arrival order; firstSpace is least-vacancy selection; run is recursion with explicit initial occupancy; park starts from empty occupancy; Success tests complete success; PF is the finite set of successful lists; M is Option-valued maximum displacement; b is the exact count; row lists the entire displacement range; UnimodalRow defines the peak property; FullConjecture quantifies that property over n>=1. row_one, row_two, row_three prove the three complete rows. success_trace proves zero-based [1,0,0] parks as [1,0,2], corresponding to one-based 211 -> 213. failure_trace proves zero-based [2,2,0] fails, corresponding to one-based 331 and failure at car 2. The Decidable instance following Success supplies the computable predicate needed for filtering and introduces no mathematical assumption.

The three row proofs and two traces use by decide, constructing kernel-checkable proof terms by reduction of the definitions. There are no sorry terms, assumed count constants, native_decide calls or additional axioms. The supplied non-authoritative preview returned verified for all 17 declared names on Lean 4.32.0, commit 8c9756b28d64dab099da31a4c09229a9e6a2ef35, with empty stderr. PF and the definitions and proofs depending on its finite enumeration use only the permitted propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The two trace proofs have no axiom dependencies. The actual feedback is retained in lean-preview.json. Central verification of the exact submitted source and independent comparison of this explanation with the declarations remain necessary. The finite checks establish no arbitrary-n unimodality conclusion.
